      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm hitting a CrashLoopBackOff on a microservice in our GKE cluster and i can't figure out why. here are the logs:\n\ncontainer_log:\n{\"level\":\"error\",\"msg\":\"failed to connect to db: connection refused\",\"timestamp\":\"2023-10-27T10:15:01Z\"}\n{\"level\":\"fatal\",\"msg\":\"shutdown initiated\",\"timestamp\":\"2023-10-27T10:15:02Z\"}\n\ncan u give me a diagnostic checklist to see if this is a resource issue, a liveness probe failure, or something in the config?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! Here's a diagnostic checklist:\n\n1. Resource Usage: Check if there is enough CPU and memory available in your GKE cluster. Use the `kubectl top` command to monitor your pod resources.\n2. Container Logs: Examine the logs from the problematic pod using the `kubectl logs <pod-name>` command to identify any resource constraints or errors.\n3. Configuration: Ensure that your pod definition and ConfigMap/Secret files are correctly configured, focusing on the environment variables and network settings relating to the database connection.\n4. Liveness Probe: Review your liveness probe configuration to ensure it is correctly set up. Validate the intervals and methods used.\n5. Database Connection: Check the status of the database service and confirm if others can connect to it. Review the firewall rules or security groups that may be blocking connection from your microservice container.\n\nTry to prioritize these steps by their likelihood and start with the most obvious ones, such as checking resources and verifying configurations.",
